{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch7JiNZ14jUoHuyd1nPek","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've written the summary from your facts and left out anything you didn't know. It's about 250 words.\n\n<final_document>\n**Tenant Defense Project: A Third Eviction Defense Attorney**\n\nLast year, 1,140 eviction cases were filed in our county. The Tenant Defense Project represented the tenant in 163 of them. We turned away about 300 more tenants because no attorney was free to take their case.\n\nOur attorneys are at capacity. Each of our two attorneys carries about 80 open cases, and one of them leaves in June.\n\nThe tenants we do represent often keep their homes or gain time to move. In 58% of last year's cases, our client either stayed in the home or secured at least 60 days to relocate.\n\nWe request $90,000 over two years ($45,000 per year) to hire a third staff attorney, so that fewer tenants are turned away for lack of an available lawyer.\n\n**Funding plan.** The position costs $78,000 per year, including benefits. The grant and a family fund's two-year pledge of $20,000 per year together cover $65,000 per year, or 83% of the cost. The Tenant Defense Project will pay the remaining $13,000 per year from its general budget. Our core operations also rely on a $60,000 annual county contract that comes up for renewal in October. Because outside funds cover most of the new position's cost, it does not depend on that renewal.\n\n**Reporting.** Each year of the grant, we will report how many tenants we represented, how many we turned away, and our case outcomes, measured the same way as the 58% figure above.\n</final_document>\n\n**Things to check before you submit:**\n\n- **Training timeline:** I didn't include the plan for the departing attorney to train the new hire. The foundation decides in May, she leaves in June, and your last hire took five months. That training only works if you hire before you know about the award. If you plan to do that, add a sentence saying so, because it would be a strong point.\n- **\"Third attorney\":** If the June vacancy won't be refilled, this grant keeps you at two attorneys instead of adding a third. Reviewers will notice. In that case, retitle it and describe the request as keeping your capacity from dropping.\n- **County contract:** I mentioned it openly and made the point that the new position mostly doesn't depend on it. Reviewers asking about sustainability will probably find out about the renewal anyway, so it's better they hear it from you. You can cut it if you'd rather.\n- **Reporting:** That paragraph is a commitment I added. Keep it only if you can track turn-aways and outcomes every year.\n- **Missing name:** Replace \"our county\" with the county's name when you have it."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1159,"cache_read_input_tokens":9979,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1159},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ddc51021-b2dd-4519-ad10-61036c669215","uuid":"ac48dfd4-024d-4419-8dfe-2208d454cf6b","timestamp":"2026-11-16T21:20:42.728Z","request_id":"req_011Ch7JiNKcoopeALFjHtuHm"}
